From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 8 Jul 1994 01:58:58 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-53] Childhood Anecdote

  A couple of sentences from my own child:
 
  - after I suggested him to fill the questions to position
himself on the ISIL's chart of views on economic and political
freedoms, he found himself in the ultra-libertarian corner of
the chart, and, being deeply impressed with the questions, asked:
 
  - "Who are those smart people?" 
 
  Then he first heard the word 'libertarians'.
 
  In a more recent case, when I felt somewhat uncomfortable about
doing some (pretty mild) thing, he soothed me:
 
  "It's not immoral, it's just illegal".
 
 
  A very remarkable feature of libertarian views (and many
other extropian concepts) is that they are easily accepted
by children as natural and are not easy to undermine.
Compare it to statist/puritan views that need long justifications
and are put to hard test by the simplest everyday temptations.
It's not accidental that communist or religious 'theorists' try
to shield their followers from the 'corruptive' outside influence
by isolating them from the world.

