From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 8 Jul 1994 02:20:13 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-54] Libertarian sales tax? (was: Extropy journal volume)

[ Lets title messages appropriately! ]

 In X-Message: #94-7-50  Mr John M Casey <jmcasey@HK.Super.NET> writes:

 
>Rather, shouldn't the cost of accomodating people to the extent they need
>be filtered somehow into the cost of the products we all buy? This would
>be a true free market approach to internalizing externalities.
 
>Now I'd also postulate that the fact of non-blind people not being blind
>is a benefit which should be paid for. If the incidence of blindness is
>one in 10,000, then 1/10,000 of the cost of providing a blind person with
>sufficient accomodation for a satisfactorily normal lifestyle should
>accrue to each non-blind person.

The incidence of non-English-speakiness among people interested in reading
Extropy may be 3:1.  Should Extropy be translated into all those languages
at the expense of English speakers?  Or only into some of them, with high
enough (?) number of people?  
  The incidence of insufficient intelligence for understanding Extropy
among humans and other objects is zillions-to-1.  Should everybody and
everything be brought up to the necessary level at the expense of Extropy
readers?   This may sound a bit extreme, but I still refuse to recognize
blind-but-smart-English-speakers as _the_ 'natural' group of recipients.

   - It appears that you suggest a *sales tax* on all products collected
by their producers, who also have to determine its size and criteria and
methods of benefits distribution.   So you are loading social/charitable
functions on economic agencies [who may be absolutely incompetent in pro-
viding them, not just unwilling).
  (It is still insufficient for handicapped people.  E.g., wheelchairs'
prices can't be subsidized by able-bodied buyers, as there are none).

  Also, how do you *make* companies do it?  By force?  Why don't you
collect the extra money yourself then and employ professionals to
establish the beneficiaries and support them with whatever they really
need? - This seems more efficient. It's taxation and socialized charity
though.

  If company owners are _free_ to do it, that sounds more libertarian.
However, an apparent improvement, both economic and social, would be
to collect as much money as you can from your market, based on your
market's economic state and nothing else, and then distribute your
personal share of the profit in the form people want to have.
Got some good profits lately from jewelry/sex toys/arts  business?
- Donate _money_ to your starving neighbors, not the products!
This is called private charity.

 

  And I definitely don't agree with the argument that my possessing any
quality somehow happens at the expense of those beings who don't have it.
Then we'd owe nothing to geniuses and everything to quarks.

