From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 08 Jul 94 00:51:34 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-56] externalities

In message #94-7-50, John M Casey wrote:

> You've brought up an interesting question,I think, one that cuts
> right to the heart of libertarian theory, and one I'm still trying
> to work out my own personal answer for. The issue is that of
> "externalities." 

Externalities are an important problem. Not because of their economic
importance, which I think is small, all things considered, but because
most libertarian theory to date has ignored them (justifiably, in my
view). But we need to eventually deal with the statist trump-card of
externalities lest we be dragged down into endless disputes about the
economic size and significance of externalities.

However,

> Now I'd also postulate that the fact of non-blind people not being
> blind is a benefit which should be paid for. 

I must completely disagree with this. To regard sight as a "benefit"
held at the expense of the blind is to deny the existence of
misfortune.  As Sasha points out, if we deny the existence of
misfortune and regard everything not disadvantageous as a special
benefit which we must pay for, we would have to pay nonliving things
for the privilege of our being alive.

Shit happens. Accidents and misfortunes do not in and of themselves
create *enforceable* obligations. (Negligence and malice do, but true
accidents do not.)

In my view, the best work done to date on the problem of externalities
is David Gauthier's MORALS BY AGREEMENT. I plan to submit a review of
this book to EXTROPY for an upcoming issue. While Gauthier *seems* to
be a Standard Average Statist (it's hard to tell from his professional
work) with only the mildest of libertarian leanings, his argument does
*nothing* to show that *involuntary* interactions are any more
effective at alleviating externalities than voluntary
interactions. And for me, the essence of libertarianism (and all
extropian political thought I've seen) has been the preference for
voluntary interaction over involuntary interaction.

I cannot quarrel with the voluntary; I must quarrel with the compulsory.

Eric Watt Forste || finger arkuat@netcom.com || http://www.c2.org/~arkuat


