From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Fri, 8 Jul 1994 03:51:17 -0100
Subject: [#94-7-57] AI development acceleration (was: Nanotechnology and uncertainty)

Robin Hanson writes

>Carl Feynman writes:
>>AI, as opposed to most other technologies, has a curious self-accelerating
>>effect.
>>
>>... Of course, the development rate cannot become infinite; eventually
>>it will be limited by some resource other than the cleverness of
>>design.  ... Note that this scenario is independent of the level of
>>nanotechnology available at the time of the development of AI.
>
>The scenario above depends crucially on the idea that there is a fixed
>amount of "intelligence" (here 200 person-years), in which any
>intelligence can design something which is twice as efficient as
>itself - can do the same design work with only half the inputs
>(including time).  In economic terms, this is like saying that the
>"production function" of intelligence has dramatic "increasing returns
>to scale".
>
>Now I'll grant that this may be possible (at least over some range),
>but you should also grant that intelligence may be much harder to
>produce than this.

Indeed you are correct.  A very good point.

>Maybe designing intelligence is so damn hard that
>smarter creatures than us find it even harder to improve on
>themselves, i.e., harder than we find it to improve on ourselves.  We
>don't really understand intelligence well enough to say one way or the
>other, and the overoptimism of previous estimates of rates of AI
>progress is not especially encouraging.

Note that I phrased my claim carefully.  This exponential runaway is not
dependent solely on building machines that are smarter in each generation,
only on building machines that are faster or cheaper.  I agree that smarter
is hard, and we have no understanding of just how hard.  But faster and
cheaper don't seem as hard to understand.  Lots of technologies show
exponential growth in capability and exponential declines in cost with each
generation, at least in the early days before they run into physical
limits.


